# 1. Background / current interpretation

The experiment series currently appears to tell the following story.

Treat this as a **hypothesis to audit**, not as established truth.

### P1–P3

Early folding strategies could reduce symbolic work, but selection/rewrite/planning overhead usually erased runtime benefits.

P3 became demand-driven but shallow recognition lost some powerful higher-level folds.

### P4

A propagated two-support semantic signature was attached while building the Boolean DAG.

This allowed ancestor folds to be recognized even when intermediate folds were not locally profitable.

On the synthetic Gate-P workload, P4 became substantially faster than direct sparse ANF **once the annotated DAG already existed**.

However, annotation construction was expensive.

### P5–P7

Several representations were tried:

- packed primitive arrays;
- metadata words stored on Python nodes;
- two `array('Q')` buffers.

These preserved the same algebraic savings but showed that changing Python storage representation alone did not eliminate source-construction overhead.

### P7B

A separate pre-dispatch DAG scan was tried.

It reduced unnecessary annotation on natural cases but was itself too expensive and was not sufficiently predictive of end-to-end profitability.

Important: P7B contains a documented timing-accounting correction. Inspect `04_EXPERIMENTS/P07B/P7B_TIMING_CORRECTION.md` and use the corrected rerun as authoritative.

### P8

A zero-pass dispatch model was introduced.

Telemetry features were accumulated while the ordinary DAG was being built.

A small frozen decision tree determined whether to use direct lowering or the P6-style propagated-fold backend.

Compiler-only results became promising, but telemetry collection itself was expensive.

### P9–P11

Telemetry was progressively simplified:

- P9 kept only primitive features actually used by the tree;
- P10 fused telemetry into the ordinary `_make` path;
- P11 reduced the frozen tree to the predicates actually necessary to reach its sole annotation leaf.

By P11 the natural-case abstention cost was near parity and Gate-P full pipeline was close to direct.

### P12

Reuse bookkeeping was made lazy.

Reuse is only exactified when the earlier predicates leave annotation possible.

Pair repeat excess can be derived as:

`binary_nodes - distinct_pairs`.

P12 reached approximately:

- case-balanced Gate-P full pipeline: ~1.02× direct;
- workload-weighted full pipeline: ~0.98× direct.

This is a secondary measured end-to-end crossover, but not the preregistered case-balanced primary crossover.

### P13

Pair counting was moved out of the per-node Python dictionary path.

Packed pair keys were appended during construction, then exact pair statistics were computed in C-backed operations at dispatch.

P13 ended up nearly tied with P12.

Current interpretation:

> The algebraic folding mechanism appears strong enough to beat direct sparse ANF. The remaining implementation gap may largely be the cost of observing pair/reuse structure in CPython.

The proposed next experiment is tentatively:

> P14A: move pair observation / telemetry into compiled code such as Cython/C while freezing the exact P13 dispatch behavior and requiring exact decision equivalence.

Your job is to determine whether that conclusion and next step are justified.

